  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Whisenhunt held them to four runs through five innings. That was plenty. The offense never caught up.",
    "what_this_means": "This is what the inconsistency looks like from the inside. Arraez had two hits and drove in two late, enough to reach four runs, but the lineup left ten men on base across nine innings and only converted runners twice. The third inning was the critical miss. Three runners in scoring position, two outs, nothing. By the time they got serious in the ninth, the lead was already built. Adrian Houser threw four clean innings after the starter left, but clean relief doesn't matter when you're always chasing. They were down one run from the first inning onward and never once took the lead. That's the shape of this team right now: capable moments wrapped around stretches of nothing, always one swing short. Ten left on base. One run to show for it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Brandon Sproat takes the mound for Milwaukee tonight at 6:45 with a 5.09 ERA, and Tyler Mahle goes for the Giants. A matchup between two arms still searching for command in the middle of a season both teams would rather move past quickly.",
